== Example: extraspecial 2-groups <sec:extraspecial>

Quillen [@bib:Quillen1971d] calculated the mod 2 cohomology of extraspecial
2-groups. We shall give a simplified version of his computation
[@bib:Benson1992b], as an example of how to apply the commutative algebra
discussed in the last section to the calculation of cohomology rings. This is
also a good example to keep in mind when reading about the Quillen
stratification in the next section.

We are interested in examining the Lyndon–Hochschild–Serre spectral
sequence#idx("Lyndon–Hochschild–Serre", "spectral sequence")#idx(
  "spectral sequence",
  "for an extraspecial 2-group",
) of a central extension
$ 1 -> N -> G -> E -> 1 $
with $N$ cyclic of order two and $E$ an elementary abelian 2-group of order
$2^n$. Strictly speaking, for $G$ to be extraspecial#idx(
  "extraspecial group",
)#idx("group", "extraspecial") one requires $N = Z(G)$ in the above situation.
However, we shall abuse terminology by using the term extraspecial even when
this condition does not hold.

We start with some preliminaries on quadratic forms. Recall that a quadratic
form#idx("quadratic form")#idx("form", "quadratic") on a vector space $V$ over a
field $k$ of characteristic two is a map $q : V -> k$ with the property that
$ q(x + y) = q(x) + q(y) + b(x, y) $
with $b : V times V -> k$ a symmetric bilinear form#idx(
  "bilinear form, symmetric",
)#idx("form", "symmetric bilinear")#idx("symmetric", "bilinear form"). A linear
subspace $W$ of $V$ is said to be isotropic#idx("isotropic", "subspace")#idx(
  "subspace, isotropic",
) if $q(w) = 0$ for all $w in W$. If $k'$ is an extension field of $k$, we
extend $q$ to a quadratic form on $k' times.o_k V$, also denoted $q$ by abuse of
notation, by setting
$
  q(sum_i lambda_i x_i) = sum_i lambda_i^2 q(x_i)
  + sum_(i < j) lambda_i lambda_j b(x_i, x_j).
$

#lemma[
  Suppose that $k$ is a perfect field of characteristic 2. Let $q$ be a
  quadratic form defined on a vector space $E$ over $FF_2$. Let
  $V = k times.o_(FF_2) E$, and denote by $F : V -> V$ the Frobenius morphism,
  corresponding to squaring elements of $k$.

  Let $h$ be the codimension in $E$ of a maximal isotropic subspace $E'$. If $v$
  is a vector in $V$ with the property that the linear span of
  $v, F(v), dots, F^(r-1) (v)$ is isotropic, with $r >= h$, then the linear span
  of $v, F(v), dots, F^r (v)$ is also isotropic.
] <lem:isotropic-frobenius-span>

#proof[
  Let $W = k times.o_(FF_2) E'$, so that $W$ is a maximal $F$-stable isotropic
  subspace of $V$. Since $r >= h$, there is a linear relation of the form
  $ w + mu_s F^s (v) + mu_(s+1) F^(s+1) (v) + dots.c + mu_t F^t (v) = 0 $
  with $w in W$, $0 <= s <= t <= r$ and $mu_s$, $mu_t$ non-zero. Since $k$ is
  perfect, $F$ is invertible and so without loss of generality $s = 0$. Then by
  applying $F^(r-t)$ #source(182)and adding if necessary, we obtain such a
  relation with $t = r$. Applying $q(-)$ and $b(w, -)$ to the above relation, we
  obtain
  $
    b(w, mu_0 v + dots.c + mu_r F^r (v))
    + q(mu_0 v + dots.c + mu_r F^r (v)) = 0 \
    b(w, mu_0 v + dots.c + mu_r F^r (v)) = 0.
  $
  Subtracting and using the fact that $q(F^i (v)) = 0$ and
  $b(F^i (v), F^j (v)) = 0$ for $|i - j| < r$, we obtain
  $ mu_0 mu_r b(v, F^r (v)) = 0. $
  Since $mu_0$ and $mu_r$ are non-zero, we deduce that $b(v, F^r (v)) = 0$,
  which completes the proof of the lemma.
]

Now, keeping the notation of the lemma, assume that $k$ is algebraically closed,
and denote by $k[V]$ the coordinate ring of $V$; namely the ring of polynomial
functions on $V$. Thus $F$ can be thought of as the $k$-linear ring homomorphism
on $k[V]$ sending each element of $E$ in degree one to its square in degree two.
So for example we can regard $q(v)$ as an element of $k[V]$ given by a degree
two polynomial, and $b(v, F^i (v))$ as an element of $k[V]$ given by a
polynomial of degree $2^i + 1$.

#proposition[
  The sequence
  $ q(v), b(v, F(v)), b(v, F^2 (v)), dots, b(v, F^(h-1) (v)) $
  is a regular sequence in $k[V]$; and $b(v, F^r (v))$ is in the radical of the
  ideal they generate, for $r >= h$. The variety defined by this ideal is the
  union of the $F$-stable isotropic subspaces of $V$; namely the union over the
  isotropic subspaces $E' <= E$ of $k times.o_(FF_2) E'$.
] <prop:isotropic-regular-sequence>

#proof[
  For $r = 0$ set $J_r = {0}$, and for $1 <= r <= infinity$ we denote by $J_r$
  the ideal in $k[V]$ generated by the sequence of elements $q(v)$,
  $b(v, F(v)), dots, b(v, F^(r-1) (v))$. Since
  $
    q(sum_(i=0)^(r-1) lambda_i F^i (v)) = sum_(i=0)^(r-1) lambda_i^2 q(v)^(2^i)
    + sum_(0 <= i < j <= r-1) lambda_i lambda_j b(v, F^(j-i) (v))^(2^i),
  $
  it follows that a vector $v in V$ lies in the subvariety $V(J_r)$ if and only
  if the linear span of $v$, $F(v), dots, F^(r-1) (v)$ is isotropic. So by the
  lemma, we have
  $ V(J_h) = V(J_(h+1)) = dots.c = V(J_infinity), $
  so that the new generators are in the radical of the ideal generated by the
  previous ones by Hilbert's Nullstellensatz (Theorem~@th:nullstellensatz). Now
  $V(J_infinity)$ is just the union of the $F$-stable isotropic subspaces of
  $V$. Some elementary linear algebra shows that an isotropic subspace of $V$ is
  $F$-stable if and only if it is of the form $k times.o_(FF_2) E'$ for some
  isotropic subspace $E' <= E$. So $V(J_infinity)$ is a subvariety of
  codimension $h$ in $V$. Since $J_h$ is generated by exactly $h$ elements, it
  follows that these elements form a regular sequence (see
  Theorem~@th:macaulay).
]

#source(183)Now returning to the above central extension
$ 1 -> N -> G -> E -> 1, $
if we write elements of $N$ and $E$ additively, we can identify $N$ with $FF_2$
and $E$ with a vector space over $FF_2$. We define a quadratic form $q : E -> N$
by taking $q(x)$ to be the square of a preimage $tilde(x)$ of $x$ in $G$. Since
commutators are central and self-inverse, we have
$
  (tilde(x) tilde(y))^2
  = tilde(x)^2 tilde(y)(tilde(y)^(-1) tilde(x)^(-1) tilde(y) tilde(x)) tilde(y)
  = tilde(x)^2 tilde(y)^2 [tilde(x), tilde(y)],
$
so the associated symmetric bilinear form $b : E times E -> N$ is given by the
commutator map on preimages in $G$.

We have $H^* (E, FF_2) = FF_2 [x_1, dots, x_n]$ with $deg(x_i) = 1$. Let $k$ be
an algebraically closed field of characteristic two, and let
$V = k times.o_(FF_2) E$, a vector space of dimension $n$ over $k$. Then
$H^* (E, k) = k[x_1, dots, x_n] = k[V]$, the coordinate ring of $V$ regarded as
an affine variety over $k$. The element of $H^2 (E, FF_2)$ classifying the above
central extension is $q(v)$. This is regarded as a polynomial function on $V$ of
degree two as follows. Let $e_1, dots, e_n$ be a basis of $E$ over $FF_2$. Then
if $v = sum_(i=1)^n x_i e_i$, we have
$
  q(v) = q(sum_(i=1)^n x_i e_i) = sum_(i=1)^n q(e_i) x_i^2
  + sum_(1 <= i < j <= n) b(e_i, e_j) x_i x_j.
$
Thus the $q(e_i)$ and $b(e_i, e_j)$ are the coefficients of $q(v)$ as a
quadratic polynomial in $k[V]$ with coefficients in $FF_2$.

Recall that the Frobenius map $F$ on $k[V]$ is defined to be the $k$-linear ring
homomorphism sending $x_i$ to $x_i^2$. Thus we have (see
Section~@sec:steenrod-operations)
$
  Sq^1 q(v) = sum_(1 <= i < j <= n) b(e_i, e_j)(x_i x_j^2 + x_j x_i^2)
  = b(v, F(v)),
$
as a polynomial of degree three in $k[V]$. Similarly for $r >= 1$ we have
$
  Sq^(2^(r-1)) Sq^(2^(r-2)) dots.c Sq^1 q(v)
  = sum_(1 <= i < j <= n) b(e_i, e_j)(x_i x_j^(2^r) + x_j x_i^(2^r))
  = b(v, F^r (v)).
$

#theorem(title: [Quillen [@bib:Quillen1971d]])[
  Let $G$ be as above. Then
  $
    H^* (G, FF_2) = FF_2 [x_1, dots, x_n] slash
    (q(v), b(v, F(v)), dots, b(v, F^(h-1) (v))) times.o FF_2 [zeta].
  $
  Here, $x_1, dots, x_n$ are degree one elements inflated from $H^* (E, FF_2)$.
  The integer $h$ is the codimension in $V$ of a maximal $F$-stable isotropic
  subspace; in other words, $2^h$ is the index in $G$ of a maximal elementary
  abelian subgroup. The element $zeta$ is any element of degree $2^h$ which
  restricts non-trivially to $H^* (N, FF_2)$.

  The elements $b(v, F^j (v))$, for $j >= h$, are in the ideal generated by the
  regular sequence $q(v), b(v, F(v)), dots, b(v, F^(h-1) (v))$.
] <th:extraspecial-cohomology>

#proof[
  We examine the Lyndon–Hochschild–Serre spectral sequence
  $ H^* (E, H^* (N, FF_2)) => H^* (G, FF_2). $
  #source(184)The $E_2$ page of this spectral sequence is
  $
    H^* (E, FF_2) times.o H^* (N, FF_2) = FF_2 [x_1, dots, x_n] times.o FF_2 [z]
  $
  with $x_1, dots, x_n in E_2^(1, 0)$ and $z in E_2^(0, 1)$. Since
  $q(v) in H^2 (E, FF_2)$ is the element corresponding to the central extension,
  we have $d_2 (z) = q(v) in E_2^(2, 0)$. Since Steenrod operations#idx(
    "spectral sequence",
    "Steenrod operations in",
  )#idx(
    "Steenrod operations",
    "in spectral sequences",
  ) commute with transgressions (Section~@sec:steenrod-spectral), we have, for
  $r >= 1$,
  $
    #aligned-rows(
      (
        $d_(2^r + 1) (z^(2^r))$,
        $= d_(2^r + 1) (Sq^(2^(r-1)) Sq^(2^(r-2)) dots.c Sq^1 (z))$,
      ),
      ($$, $= Sq^(2^(r-1)) Sq^(2^(r-2)) dots.c Sq^1 q(v) = b(v, F^r (v)).$),
    )
  $
  Now by Proposition~@prop:isotropic-regular-sequence, the elements
  $q(v), b(v, F(v)), dots, b(v, F^(h-1) (v))$ form a regular sequence in
  $H^* (E, FF_2)$, and so by induction on $r$ we have, for $1 <= r <= h$ and
  $2^(r-1) + 1 < j <= 2^r + 1$,
  $
    E_j^(* *) = FF_2 [x_1, dots, x_n] slash
    (q(v), b(v, F(v)), dots, b(v, F^(r-1) (v))) times.o FF_2 [z^(2^r)].
  $
  To complete the calculation, it suffices to show that the element
  $z^(2^h) in E_2^(0, 2^h)$ is a universal cycle. Let $H$ be a maximal
  elementary abelian subgroup of $G$, of index $2^h$. Then $z$ is a restriction
  of an element $hat(z) in H^1 (H, FF_2)$. One then easily checks using the
  Mackey formula for Evens' norm map (Section~@sec:evens-norm), that
  $norm_(H, G) (hat(z))$ is an element of $H^* (G, FF_2)$ restricting to
  $z^(2^h) in H^* (N, FF_2)$. Thus $z^(2^h)$ is a universal cycle. In
  particular, for $j >= h$, $d_(2^j + 1) (z^(2^j)) = 0$, and so $b(v, F^j (v))$
  is in the ideal generated by $q(v), b(v, F(v)), dots, b(v, F^(h-1) (v))$.
]

#exercises[
  + Let $G$ be as above. Show that the $E_2$ page of the Eilenberg–Moore
    spectral sequence (see Sections~@sec:eilenberg-moore and
    @sec:steenrod-spectral) converging to $H^* (G, FF_2)$ is equal to the
    $E_infinity$ page, and has
    $ FF_2 [x_1, dots, x_n] slash (q(v), b(v, F(v)), dots, b(v, F^(h-1) (v))) $
    on the vertical axis.
  + (Avrunin and Carlson#idx("Avrunin", "–Carlson example")) Let $G$ be the
    nilpotent 2-group of class two and order $2^(2n)$ generated by elements
    $g_1, dots, g_n$ subject to the relations $g_i^4 = 1$,
    $[g_i, g_(i+1)] = g_(i+1)^2$, and $[g_i, g_j] = 1$ if $j >= i + 2$. Let $N$
    be the central subgroup of order $2^n$ generated by $g_1^2, dots, g_n^2$,
    and let $E = G slash N$. Show that the Lyndon–Hochschild–Serre spectral
    sequence for the central extension $1 -> N -> G -> E -> 1$ satisfies
    $E_3 = E_infinity$. Show that there is an element $x$ of $H^1 (G, FF_p)$
    with $x^n != 0$ but $x^(n+1) = 0$.
]
